1
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:09,937
The world.

2
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,877
What a glorious place.

3
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:16,117
Seek freedom, and it will lie
stretched out before your eyes.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,340
If the endless dream guides
your restless spirit,

5
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:23,960
seize it, raise your flag, and stand tall.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,717
I think back to the time
when my search first began.

7
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,317
I left behind those shores of sand.

8
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,700
For the endless everlasting
world out there.

9
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:42,817
I know that with this
burning passion driving me.

10
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:48,540
Someday, somewhere, I'll finally see the
light I have searched for everywhere?

11
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:55,757
When the summer sun is shining.

12
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,557
Shaking the sails of my heart. I know.

13
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:04,437
A brand new world

14
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:05,817
that has been hiding.

15
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,917
Has opened up its doors for me to go.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,900
A way from the moon's sky.

17
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:15,377
Wearing in the troubled waves.

18
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:17,877
From all despair I can be saved.

19
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,177
For the horizon on the other side is.

20
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:23,477
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

21
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:25,577
Is what I am for?

22
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:28,660
And though I travel far
from where my search began,

23
00:01:28,780 --> 00:01:31,237
over seas of green and shores of sand,

24
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,560
through the endless
everlasting world out there.

25
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:38,780
I know that with this
burning passion driving me,

26
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:44,200
some days somewhere I'll finally see
the light I will search for everywhere.

27
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:54,777
My money says that we lived through this.

28
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:59,213
A cloud road that goes straight up,
you might want to reconsider that bet.

29
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,036
I wonder if this is a natural
formation of the clouds.

30
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,671
There's nothing natural about this at all.

31
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:20,300
Look, the exit!

32
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,220
They put up a big sign
showing us! Godland?

33
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:25,840
What is Godland Skypiea?

34
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:29,440
This isn't the exit!

35
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:30,780
It's the entrance!

36
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:41,400
Ha ha ha!

37
00:02:57,700 --> 00:02:59,260
There it is!

38
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:00,980
Sky Island!

39
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:13,160
Wait, didn't the sign
we passed say Skypiea?

40
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:18,465
Yeah, that's the same name scribbled
on the map that Lock found.

41
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:23,245
So, I guess that galleon that
fell on us actually did come here.

42
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:26,680
The last time they set eyes
on this was 200 years ago.

43
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:30,549
I'm amazed that this place has
stayed beautiful for so long.

44
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:39,080
You can smell the adventure.

45
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:41,364
Putin is the seaweed in summer.

46
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:43,557
Wait for me! I like adventure!

47
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:44,875
It's kind of shallow.

48
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:49,860
Usopp, did you hurt yourself? Hello?

49
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,557
Hey, is it just me or is this
whole place made of clouds?

50
00:03:57,640 --> 00:03:59,060
Big fluffy ones.

51
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:00,480
I love this stuff.

52
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,780
Come on, let's go ashore. Yeah.

53
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:09,960
Wait, I'm coming with you guys.

54
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:12,437
Me too, but I got to change first.

55
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:15,597
Luffy, we may have a problem
with dropping anchor here.

56
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:17,577
There's no seabed at the
bottom of this cloud.

57
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:20,277
You know, I thought about
that, and who cares?

58
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:22,480
I love the way you think. Thanks.

59
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:26,695
Yeah, I want to play.

60
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:32,037
I'll butt down and check
this out, you guys.

61
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:33,826
The beach is actually fluffy.

62
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,697
It squishes between my
toes like marshmallow mud.

63
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:38,597
What the heck is marshmallow mud?

64
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:39,937
I don't know, but that's what it's like.

65
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:44,740
Well, regardless of how the ground
may feel, it sure is an amazing sight.

66
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:46,429
Better than I imagined it.

67
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:51,120
It's a piece of work for sure, but I
think our shipmates are overreacting.

68
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:54,578
Bugs me when they get all worked up.

69
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:58,507
When they get all worked up.

70
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:01,697
You're hurting me.

71
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:02,898
I said I was sorry.

72
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:08,657
We forgot about the South Bird.

73
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:10,760
We really should have let him go.

74
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,620
He'll never survive here.

75
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:14,096
I wouldn't sweat it.

76
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,377
Birds adapt very quickly
to their environments.

77
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:19,077
And if not, there seem to be
some people living up here.

78
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:20,317
Don't worry about it.

79
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:21,230
He'll be fine.

80
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:22,860
Yeah, I guess.

81
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:24,440
Hey, the anchor!

82
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:25,820
All taken care of.

83
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,757
It hit the heavier cloud below, which
is what I think this place floats on.

84
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:33,077
That means that Sky Island
floats on a sea of cloud cover.

85
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:36,817
I wonder if it's stationary in the
sky or if it moves with the tide.

86
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:40,488
We could sit on deck all day
and pick this whole thing apart.

87
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:42,800
Come on, what fun would that be?

88
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:47,459
Fact is, most of this makes zero
sense, so let's make the best of it.

89
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:55,737
This is why we came here, you guys!

90
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:57,440
Welcome to Sky Island!

91
00:05:58,760 --> 00:05:59,600
Come on!

92
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:01,640
If I can do this, so can you!

93
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:02,760
Just jump!

94
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:05,197
Swordsman, you joining us?

95
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:06,110
Yeah.

96
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:08,018
I'll be along soon.

97
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:12,980
The captain smells adventure. Huh.

98
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:17,119
I never stop to think of
any of this as adventurous.

99
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:20,618
Maybe I should reconsider my stance.

100
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:31,644
Uh, something about the squishiness of
this island makes me want to sleep all day.

101
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:34,250
Ahoy!

102
00:06:34,333 --> 00:06:35,637
This is your captain speaking!

103
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:36,550
King,

104
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:38,017
I must inform you that
this place is too much

105
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,806
fun for me to make any
more captain decisions.

106
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:42,077
Aye, aye, captain.

107
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:44,617
What do you say we just
sit around and do nothing?

108
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:45,530
Huh?

109
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:48,700
Nothing sounds like a plan to me. Denied!

110
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,600
But denied means no!

111
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:53,040
Such mysterious-looking flowers.

112
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,239
I've never seen anything like them.

113
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:01,417
I wonder if they're balloonish
like that sea monster.

114
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,147
They'll pop when I pick one.

115
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:08,920
Uh, this is great.

116
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:13,260
Those marine losers can't follow
us up here so we can all cut loose.

117
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:19,660
Hey, Chopper, what do you
think that is over there?

118
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:21,730
What?

119
00:07:21,813 --> 00:07:23,000
All right, Mr.

120
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:25,340
Giant Fruit, you're all mine.

121
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:29,160
Oh, that hurt.

122
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:31,100
Why is this thing so hard?

123
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:33,360
Hey, Usopp. Yeah?

124
00:07:33,580 --> 00:07:34,933
Got some fruit for you.

125
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:37,857
You practically crammed
my brain into my chest!

126
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:39,234
What's wrong with you?

127
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:42,500
Wait up, Robin, baby!

128
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:44,888
I've got a present for you.

129
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:46,140
An island flower.

130
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,580
And one for...

131
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:50,570
Nami!

132
00:07:50,653 --> 00:07:52,420
Nami, baby, where'd you run off to?

133
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:54,000
I've got a gift for you!

134
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:58,377
I can't believe I want
a chair made of cloud.

135
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:01,397
I wonder if they have a factory
that makes all this stuff.

136
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:03,777
This cloud is different
from the stuff on the beach.

137
00:08:03,860 --> 00:08:05,213
It's a lot of fun, too.

138
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:09,276
I didn't figure on getting
wet walking through clouds.

139
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:11,080
What's that?

140
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:19,350
Sorry.

141
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:22,620
What do we have here?

142
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:23,820
Some kind of fox.

143
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:28,700
Is that music?

144
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:32,270
Look.

145
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:33,617
There's someone over there.

146
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:34,530
On that cloud.

147
00:08:34,980 --> 00:08:35,860
Oh, great.

148
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:37,400
It's another monster.

149
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,390
Where's the whistle?

150
00:08:38,473 --> 00:08:39,303
Where's the whistle?

151
00:08:39,386 --> 00:08:41,057
We can ask the Second to save us.

152
00:08:41,140 --> 00:08:42,360
No, we can't.

153
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:43,957
That's not who you think it is.

154
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,060
That right there is an angel.

155
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:48,880
An angel?

156
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:55,650
Esso?

157
00:08:57,300 --> 00:08:58,130
Esso?

158
00:08:58,213 --> 00:09:00,260
Sue, come here. Sue?

159
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:02,737
Did you come from the Blue Sea World?

160
00:09:02,820 --> 00:09:06,677
If that's what you call it, we sealed
up a river of clouds to get here.

161
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:08,878
Speaking of here, is this your home?

162
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:10,480
Yes, it sure is.

163
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:15,139
And I'd like to welcome you
to Skypia's Angel Beach.

164
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:17,824
I bet you'd like to drink that.

165
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:23,660
The top of a konash is as hard as steel.

166
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:28,628
It's impossible to crack, so you have
to cut through the underside, like this.

167
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:31,820
Here you go.

168
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:40,658
If it's that good, I gotta try some!

169
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:42,113
Come on, give it to me!

170
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:48,360
My name is Conis, and
this little one is Sue.

171
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:49,599
She's a cloud fox.

172
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:53,077
If you need anything at all,
please let me be of service.

173
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:54,280
Yeah, about that.

174
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:56,840
Your eyes are setting my heart on...

175
00:09:57,780 --> 00:09:58,610
Lay off.

176
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:01,855
Actually, we've got tons of questions.

177
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:05,717
I hate to bother you, but we've
never been anyplace like this before.

178
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:08,900
Sure, ask me anything you
want to know. Guys, look.

179
00:10:09,180 --> 00:10:10,298
Something's coming.

180
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:12,220
Oh, that's my father.

181
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:14,000
House, Conis!

182
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:15,920
House, father!

183
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:19,054
Can't you say hello like normal people?

184
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:22,657
Conis, what's that thing he's riding on?

185
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:25,080
Ah, that would be my father's waver.

186
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,080
Whoa, that's awesome!

187
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:29,660
A sky waver.

188
00:10:30,860 --> 00:10:31,690
Gangway!

189
00:10:31,773 --> 00:10:33,693
Stopping this thing is a bit of a trick!

190
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:43,660
My goodness, is everyone all right?

191
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:45,488
You're the one who crashed!

192
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:50,017
Hey, Luffy, didn't you bring back
something like that waver from the seabed?

193
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:51,629
Oh, yeah, I did, didn't I?

194
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:56,617
Then that must be the boat we read
about in that Noland the Liar book,

195
00:10:56,700 --> 00:10:58,597
a boat that can sail without wind!

196
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:01,940
Did you make some new
friends? Conis, my dear? Yes.

197
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:05,357
They arrived only a few minutes
ago from the Blue Sea World.

198
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:06,616
Oh, still adjusting.

199
00:11:07,020 --> 00:11:10,297
There must be a great many things that
puzzle you about the White White Sea.

200
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:11,210
I'm sorry.

201
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:12,957
There's no need to apologize.

202
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:15,457
Forgive me for not
introducing myself sooner.

203
00:11:15,540 --> 00:11:16,599
My name is Pagaya.

204
00:11:16,780 --> 00:11:18,604
Oh, it's very nice to meet you.

205
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:21,115
So, Father, how was the fishing today?

206
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:22,888
Quite a successful venture.

207
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:24,780
I have an idea.

208
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,757
You should all come over for dinner.

209
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:29,237
We'll fill your bellies with
the taste of the island.

210
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:31,380
Come out for that! I'm starving!

211
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:33,260
Sky Island Cuisine?

212
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:35,197
Do you mind if I help you prepare it?

213
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:36,110
No.

214
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:38,542
Before we go, I have a question.

215
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:41,617
This boat, it doesn't have
any sails to catch the wind,

216
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:43,217
and you weren't rowing it either.

217
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:45,760
So, how's it propelled through the water?

218
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:47,020
Oh, of course.

219
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:50,057
Your people aren't familiar
with dials, are they?

220
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:51,340
Order dials!

221
00:11:53,820 --> 00:11:54,650
Ah!

222
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:04,650
Oh, no.

223
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:06,234
I hope he's all right.

224
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:07,560
It's all my fault.

225
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:10,407
I shouldn't have let a
rookie use the waiver.

226
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:14,382
Since Luffy can't swim, I hope
this sea is more buoyant than ours.

227
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:15,750
Maybe.

228
00:12:15,833 --> 00:12:18,317
If it can stay floating in the
air, then you're probably right.

229
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:19,400
We can handle it.

230
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:22,650
He sank.

231
00:12:22,820 --> 00:12:24,257
That's not the first time I've been wrong.

232
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:25,170
Ah!

233
00:12:25,253 --> 00:12:26,083
I'll save you!

234
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:29,020
That was close. Close?

235
00:12:29,300 --> 00:12:30,677
You were standing on the beach.

236
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:32,157
What do you know about close?

237
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,857
Oh, yeah, and thanks for
all the help, by the way.

238
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:37,705
Is it really necessary
for you to jump in, too?

239
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:38,717
Sky Island's scary!

240
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:39,918
Sky Island's scary!

241
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:43,317
I apologize for letting
him take the waver out.

242
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:46,377
With its light body, even a
small wave can shake the rudder.

243
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:49,337
Unless you know the white, white
sea like the back of your hand,

244
00:12:49,420 --> 00:12:51,714
the waver is almost impossible to ride.

245
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:53,200
But Ken, I am sorry.

246
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,477
It's only recently that I
became good enough to ride it.

247
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,266
Whoa, is it really that hard?

248
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:01,600
It usually takes ten years
of training to drive it.

249
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:03,132
You've got to be joking!

250
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:04,740
Wow, look at her go!

251
00:13:05,380 --> 00:13:06,620
This is wonderful!

252
00:13:06,860 --> 00:13:09,377
Oh, Nami, you're a
vision riding that waver!

253
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:10,417
That's incredible.

254
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:11,597
I can't believe it.

255
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:12,917
This is her first time.

256
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,412
Yeah, how come she can do it and I can't?

257
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:17,840
Wow, this really requires skill.

258
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:20,456
No wonder you had a hard time, Luffy.

259
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:27,500
Nami, we're invited to dinner, so get
off that thing and stop being so rude!

260
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:29,121
Talk about rude.

261
00:13:29,340 --> 00:13:31,560
Stupid Waverhog! Go on ahead.

262
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:34,777
If it's okay with Peak, yeah, I'd
like to stay out here and ride.

263
00:13:34,860 --> 00:13:36,640
That's fine. Just be careful.

264
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:39,717
Riding this freely on
the open ocean without

265
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:42,153
worrying about the wind is like a dream.

266
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,457
I wonder if we can use these
on our ocean back home.

267
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:47,880
Whoa, is that some kind of factory?

268
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:52,520
Indeed it is.

269
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:54,640
That is our cloud quarry site.

270
00:13:54,880 --> 00:13:59,117
It's where our clouds are manufactured
and cut to meet all types of needs.

271
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:00,877
I didn't know you could cut a cloud.

272
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:04,477
You did take the Milky Road to get
to the White White Sea, correct?

273
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:05,440
Milky Road?

274
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:07,837
Is that the name of that messed
up current we took up here?

275
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:08,750
Yes.

276
00:14:08,833 --> 00:14:10,617
It's a canal constructed by our engineers

277
00:14:10,700 --> 00:14:13,288
and made using the
natural clouds we quarry.

278
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:16,817
There are actually two types of
natural clouds on our island.

279
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:19,597
Your ship traveled atop
what we call sea clouds?

280
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:21,397
And the ones that float on their own?

281
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:23,177
That we can stand on and build on?

282
00:14:23,260 --> 00:14:24,780
Are called island clouds.

283
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:28,957
They're not the normal clouds
that we're used to, are they?

284
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:31,677
No Their condensation
nucleus is different!

285
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:33,289
From what you usually see?

286
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:38,077
It has to do with a mineral found in the
blue sea world Called sea prism stone.

287
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:41,513
Sea prism stone What effect
does that have on the clouds?

288
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:42,470
It's like this.

289
00:14:42,553 --> 00:14:45,077
Inside the sea prism
are carnelian particles.

290
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:46,297
Called pyrobrine.

291
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:51,520
During volcanic eruptions, these particles
gain moisture and descend into the sky.

292
00:14:52,120 --> 00:14:55,591
Our clouds are formed based on
the density of the moisture.

293
00:14:55,700 --> 00:14:57,876
Oh, yeah, I heard about those things.

294
00:14:58,620 --> 00:14:59,450
Sure!

295
00:14:59,533 --> 00:15:00,877
I remember that from when I was a kid.

296
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:04,177
We used to always play with
carnelian particles in the yard.

297
00:15:04,260 --> 00:15:05,560
Enough sarcasm!

298
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:10,777
In all actuality, there are three types
of clouds that we use in our day-to-day.

299
00:15:10,860 --> 00:15:12,997
The sea and island types are natural.

300
00:15:13,080 --> 00:15:14,977
The man-made ones come from island clouds.

301
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:17,160
Now, if you'll follow me, please.

302
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:20,580
Check out the view from up here. Look!

303
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:23,237
If you squint, you can
see Nami riding the waver.

304
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:25,144
Luffy, what are you waiting on?

305
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:28,628
How come she's so good
at that stupid thing?

306
00:15:29,740 --> 00:15:30,620
Fall off!

307
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:31,740
Cut it out.

308
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:32,857
Thanks, Sonja.

309
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:34,357
You just made me bite my tongue.

310
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:36,020
I'm bleeding. Poor baby.

311
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:37,777
Hey, Pagaya, which way to the kitchen?

312
00:15:37,860 --> 00:15:38,978
It's right up here.

313
00:15:44,420 --> 00:15:45,840
What happened to you?

314
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:50,840
Well, I saw this stuff sitting
in your fridge and I, uh,

315
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:56,460
I'd never seen anything like it, so I
tried it and, and it tastes like death.

316
00:15:56,700 --> 00:15:59,077
Now I'm all dizzy and
my tongue feels swollen.

317
00:15:59,160 --> 00:15:59,990
How's it look?

318
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:02,097
Like you drank some spoiled sauce.

319
00:16:02,180 --> 00:16:04,337
Then why did you leave
this stuff sitting around?

320
00:16:04,420 --> 00:16:07,200
Sounds like you're having
fun in the kitchen.

321
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,955
I bet Sanji's just excited about Sky
Island food being such a new thing.

322
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,640
So, uh, I was wondering,

323
00:16:14,940 --> 00:16:19,137
could you explain some more to us about
these dials you mentioned earlier?

324
00:16:19,220 --> 00:16:20,050
Sure.

325
00:16:20,133 --> 00:16:20,963
But it's complex.

326
00:16:21,046 --> 00:16:23,158
I don't exactly know where to start.

327
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:28,600
Ah, I've never been this relaxed.

328
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:30,050
Hmm.

329
00:16:30,133 --> 00:16:33,097
This doesn't look like a
dial, it's just a seashell.

330
00:16:33,180 --> 00:16:37,298
Yes, at first glance, but why don't
you put it to your mouth and talk?

331
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:38,400
Usopp Sting!

332
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:40,366
Why do you always pick on me?

333
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:43,989
Now, just press down
the apex of the shell.

334
00:16:45,560 --> 00:16:47,380
Wanna tell me what an apex is?

335
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:49,539
It means vertex, our highest point.

336
00:16:50,100 --> 00:16:50,980
Usopp Sting!

337
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:53,797
The seashell's making fun of Usopp too!

338
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:55,577
What did I ever do to you, seashell?

339
00:16:55,660 --> 00:16:57,077
Now, just press down
the apex of the shell.

340
00:16:57,160 --> 00:16:58,660
Hey, that's a neat trick.

341
00:16:58,960 --> 00:17:01,157
So we can store any
sound and play it back?

342
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:02,817
Then that shell is one of the dials.

343
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:05,600
Yes, we call this one a tone dial.

344
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:10,784
It's a shell from the white, white sea
that records and replays whatever it hears.

345
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:12,260
Hey, hold on.

346
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:14,397
I didn't think there was
a bottom to your sea.

347
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:16,237
Where do these things live, anyway?

348
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:18,967
They live in the shallows
in the cloud reefs.

349
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:23,037
I still don't see how that little thing
could move your father's sky waver.

350
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:26,277
Well, that's because this is
the kind that powers the waver.

351
00:17:26,360 --> 00:17:29,337
It's smaller, but it does
demonstrate the same process.

352
00:17:29,420 --> 00:17:30,714
Here, let me show you.

353
00:17:34,900 --> 00:17:36,897
So this one blows wind
out of its backside.

354
00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:38,000
Let me see.

355
00:17:39,880 --> 00:17:41,377
This is called a breath dial.

356
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:45,695
It allows you the ability to store
the wind and expel it at your desire.

357
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:51,177
The wind capacity varies depending
on the size of the shell.

358
00:17:51,260 --> 00:17:55,617
If you attach the right size to the stern
of a ship You can move along swiftly.

359
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:57,994
I've never heard of anything like that.

360
00:17:59,180 --> 00:18:00,297
I get it now.

361
00:18:00,380 --> 00:18:05,440
The waver moved by blowing a stream of
wind through the water that waver is fun.

362
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:08,645
Dials don't just power wavers.

363
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:12,197
There's also the skating
kind of the board kind But I

364
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:14,977
don't go for those the wavers
about as crazy as I get.

365
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:17,537
I wish I was out there on
that thing instead of Nami.

366
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:20,477
And I could be if I could figure
out how to fix the one we have.

367
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:22,877
How did you ever find
a waver in your world?

368
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:25,977
Well, it's kind of hard to
even tell if that's what it is.

369
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:28,637
You know that hunk of
junk is 200 years old

370
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,877
in a total wreck There's no way it works.

371
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:32,548
That might not be the case.

372
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:36,417
Dials are made from the
remains of dead shellfish.

373
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:39,657
They can last indefinitely so long
as the shell itself isn't broken.

374
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:40,817
You might get lucky.

375
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:41,820
I told you so!

376
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:43,317
You never said anything like that.

377
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:44,230
I know.

378
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:47,457
I'd venture a guess there
are many types of dials.

379
00:18:47,540 --> 00:18:48,620
Is this one here?

380
00:18:48,820 --> 00:18:50,440
Yes, that's a lamp dial.

381
00:18:52,060 --> 00:18:52,890
Heat!

382
00:18:52,973 --> 00:18:54,014
It lit up from inside!

383
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:55,330
Right!

384
00:18:55,413 --> 00:18:57,357
There are flame dials
that store heat and fire,

385
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:00,237
Flavor dials that store
various tastes and aromas.

386
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:03,137
And vision dials that store
everything the user sees.

387
00:19:03,220 --> 00:19:04,697
Which you can watch again and again?

388
00:19:04,780 --> 00:19:08,180
I want to live in the clouds
for the rest of my life!

389
00:19:08,820 --> 00:19:09,860
To be honest,

390
00:19:10,020 --> 00:19:13,817
we couldn't really sustain life here
in the clouds without these dials.

391
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:16,500
Our culture has evolved with the dials.

392
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:21,045
I suppose in the same way they
evolved with the white white sea.

393
00:19:30,280 --> 00:19:32,357
Ladies and gentlemen, dinner is served.

394
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:34,477
We have for you Sky
Lobster with special sauce

395
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:36,737
and a side of island fruits
that I can't pronounce.

396
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:37,938
That looks so good!

397
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:39,140
Let's dig in!

398
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:43,960
Oh, wow, it tastes sweet!

399
00:19:44,440 --> 00:19:46,852
I wouldn't have expected it from lobster.

400
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:48,130
Yum!

401
00:19:53,180 --> 00:19:55,737
Hey, guys, any of you know where Nami is?

402
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:57,644
I think she's riding the Waver.

403
00:19:58,020 --> 00:19:59,077
I don't see her.

404
00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:00,697
She probably went out too far.

405
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:01,610
She's like that.

406
00:20:01,693 --> 00:20:02,523
Who cares?

407
00:20:02,606 --> 00:20:04,026
You should have some of this.

408
00:20:04,620 --> 00:20:05,460
I'm worried.

409
00:20:05,740 --> 00:20:06,957
Do you think she's okay?

410
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:10,393
I have my concerns, but I
can't say one way or the other.

411
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:13,008
What are you talking about?

412
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:14,177
In the clouds,

413
00:20:14,260 --> 00:20:18,177
there's a place called the Upper Yard that
no one is ever allowed to set foot on.

414
00:20:18,260 --> 00:20:19,560
It's not too far away.

415
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:22,937
It would only be a short
trip by Waver from here.

416
00:20:23,020 --> 00:20:27,314
I'm afraid your friend Nami might have
set ashore there out of curiosity.

417
00:20:29,940 --> 00:20:31,100
They're huge!

418
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:32,840
What is this?

419
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:36,897
What's so bad that no one can
ever go over to this island?

420
00:20:36,980 --> 00:20:38,333
Is it full of monsters?

421
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:41,480
It's sacred ground. Sacred ground?

422
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:44,860
But it's so much more than just that.

423
00:20:46,640 --> 00:20:49,240
It's the island where God lives.

424
00:20:53,020 --> 00:20:53,850
Thank you.
